# CHANGELOG.md deliberately has NO merge driver. This file records why, because
# the obvious fix is to give it `merge=union` and that was tried here and removed.
#
# THE PROBLEM UNION WAS ADDED FOR, which is real: two branches that each add a
# bullet under the same `[Unreleased]` heading are not disagreeing — they are
# inserting at the same position, which git's default driver calls a conflict.
# Measured 2026-09-04: of 49 open pull requests, 21 could not merge, and 18 of
# those 21 conflicted on CHANGELOG.md and nothing else.
#
# WHY UNION IS NOT THE FIX. `union` takes lines from BOTH sides inside a
# conflicting hunk. It therefore cannot express a REMOVAL: when one side deletes
# lines that the other side's hunk overlaps, the deleted lines come back. That
# is not a corner case here — cutting a release MOVES every unreleased entry
# under a version heading, so every branch open across a release cut is exposed.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-10, on a real open pull request:
#   * A release cut removed 287 substantive lines from CHANGELOG.md.
#   * A branch merged the base afterwards with an ordinary `git merge`.
#   * The merge reported NO conflict and exited 0.
#   * All 287 removed lines were present in the branch afterwards.
# Replayed in isolation on the same inputs: the plain three-way merge exits 1
# (the real conflict), the union merge exits 0, and the union output contains 78
# unique substantive lines that the base had deleted. A probe line appears once
# in the merge base, zero times in the base branch, zero times in changelog.d/,
# and once in the union output.
#
# THE PART THAT DECIDED IT: that pull request was fully COMPLIANT. It used
# changelog.d/ fragments and never edited CHANGELOG.md deliberately. Union hit it
# anyway, during a routine catch-up merge. A hazard you cannot avoid by following
# the convention is not a hazard a convention can contain.
#
# AND THE BENEFIT WAS ALREADY MEASURED AT ZERO for the case that matters. GitHub
# ignores a repository's .gitattributes in its server-side merge — measured
# against GitHub's own merge engine (POST /repos/:owner/:repo/merges) on two
# branch pairs built to collide on this file: 409 Conflict WITH the union
# attribute present in both commits, and 409 for the no-attribute control. So the
# rule provably could not make a conflicting PR mergeable on github.com. It
# bought local convenience only, and paid for it with silent data loss at exit 0.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY FIXES THE COLLISION: one fragment per change, in changelog.d/,
# assembled at release time by scripts/assemble_changelog.py. Two branches
# writing two different filenames have nothing to merge. That is the structural
# fix and it is already in place; union was a stopgap layered on top of it.
#
# ONE RESIDUAL WINDOW, which no commit can close: a branch created while union
# was in force still carries `merge=union` in its CHECKED-OUT .gitattributes,
# and attributes resolve from the checkout — so that branch's FIRST catch-up
# merge after this file lands still union-merges CHANGELOG.md and can still
# resurrect release-cut deletions, at exit 0, one last time. Verified 2026-09-21
# on a synthetic repo: a branch with the attribute merged a commit deleting
# lines inside a hunk the branch edited and removing the attribute — the deleted
# lines returned, exit 0, no conflict. The remedy is the same one the last
# paragraph names: after the merge, diff CHANGELOG.md against the base's and
# restore the removals. Every merge after that first one is safe.
#
# SO: a CHANGELOG.md collision now CONFLICTS, visibly, and is resolved by hand —
# which for a branch carrying an inline entry means taking the base's file and
# moving the entry into a fragment. A visible conflict is the correct outcome:
# the failure mode this file exists to prevent is the SILENT one.
#
# tests/test_changelog_merge_attribute.py pins all of this by behaviour — that
# the collision conflicts, that union would resurrect deletions, and that NO
# tracked path resolves to a union driver.
